</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Additional details on end-to-end operator performance</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval overhead vs. sequence length.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our operator consists of (i) a lightweight retrieval stage that computes segment-wise ranking indices
(e.g., sorted key offsets and an optional query permutation), followed by (ii) the sparse attention computation.
In our implementation, the dominant retrieval cost comes from scoring keys using per-segment query summaries,
which scales approximately as <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L^{2}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L^{2}/S)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence length and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the segment length.
The remaining parts (e.g., local query scoring and intra-segment sorting) contribute lower-order terms close to
<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> (and a small <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L\log S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L\log S)</annotation></semantics></math> sorting cost).
Therefore, increasing <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> reduces retrieval overhead roughly inversely, while increasing <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> amplifies it superlinearly.
In practice, we use a smaller segment length for short sequences to increase sparsity, and switch to a larger segment length
as <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> grows to reduce retrieval time, since at ultra-long contexts the sparsity ratio is often already very high and
the retrieval overhead can become non-negligible relative to the sparse attention computation itself.
Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Retrieval overhead vs. sequence length. ‣ C.2 Additional details on end-to-end operator performance ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ S2O: Early Stopping for Sparse Attention via Online Permutation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the measured retrieval latency (ms) across <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,S)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
